---
// Policy: accessibility, what the site does (science.css, the markup of the pages), what is known
// not to work well yet, and how to report a barrier. No formal audit has been made: none is claimed.
import Info from "../../components/Info.astro";
import { SITE_NAME, SOURCE_URL } from "../../config";

const toc = [
  { id: "aim", text: "The aim" },
  { id: "does", text: "What the site does" },
  { id: "limits", text: "Known limits" },
  { id: "report", text: "Reporting a barrier" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Accessibility"
  description={`What ${SITE_NAME} does for accessibility, its known limits, and how to report a barrier.`}
  group="policies"
  path="/policies/accessibility/"
  parents={[{ text: "Policies", href: "/policies/" }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    We want everyone who reads research to be able to read its code on {SITE_NAME}. The site aims at the Web Content
    Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2, level AA. It has not been audited against them and does not claim to conform.
    This page says what it does and what is known not to work well yet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="aim">The aim</h2>
  <p>
    The site is made of plain pages, built ahead of time, with one stylesheet and the structure of a scientific archive:
    headings, lists, tables and words. It keeps widgets to a minimum and does not rely on colour.
  </p>

  <h2 id="does">What the site does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The language of each page is declared; each page has one main heading, then headings
      in order; the masthead, the breadcrumb, the main content and the footer are landmarks; lists are lists and tables
      have headers.</li>
    <li>A paper's status is said in words, and not by a colour or a badge alone. The Code ↔ Paper reader lists every
      match in words, beside its colours.</li>
    <li>Every link, button and field can be reached and used with the keyboard, with a visible
      focus outline; the reader's panes can be focused and scrolled.</li>
    <li>Every form field has a label; results and errors are announced to screen readers
      (<code>role="status"</code>, <code>role="alert"</code>); the removal request is sent only after you review it.</li>
    <li>The pages reflow down to a phone's width without scrolling sideways,
      and the browser's zoom enlarges everything; long names, addresses and titles wrap.</li>
    <li>Text on the default ground meets a contrast of 4.5:1; the code's syntax colours keep
      4.5:1 on every shade of the reader's matches.</li>
    <li>The only animation, a loading bar, stops when your system asks for reduced motion.</li>
    <li>The logo and the badge have text alternatives; the site has no image that carries
      information without words.</li>
    <li>Every static page (a paper's record, the lists, the categories, the information pages) can be read whole without
      JavaScript.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">Known limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The search, the DOI lookup, the pages of authors, journals, institutions, tools and datasets, the forms and the
      reader's paper pane need JavaScript.</li>
    <li>The Code ↔ Paper reader (two panes that scroll on their own, colour-paired highlights) is the most complex part
      of the site and has not been tested with screen readers in depth; the list of matches and the file's source links
      are the accessible way through it.</li>
    <li>The six colours of the matches are told apart by colour only on the page; their order and number are in the list
      of matches.</li>
    <li>The paper's text in the reader is shown as plain text: its figures, tables and equations are not reproduced; the
      reader links to the paper.</li>
    <li>The base text size is set in pixels: the browser's zoom enlarges it, but a default text size chosen in the
      browser's settings does not.</li>
    <li>Content comes from papers and repositories as their authors wrote them: a title's formula may read awkwardly, a
      script may be uncommented.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="report">Reporting a barrier</h2>
  <p>
    Open an issue on the <a href={`${SOURCE_URL}/issues`}>project's GitHub</a>, saying the page, what you tried to do, and
    what you use (browser, assistive technology). {SITE_NAME} has no email address. The operator reads the project's
    issues; a fix goes into the public history of the code.
  </p>
</Info>
